.TH WEIRD 1 "January 22, 2026" "weird 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
weird \- process anomaly detector for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B weird
[\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-\-net\fR] [\fB\-\-net\-outbound\-only\fR]
[\fB\-\-include\-listen\fR] [\fB\-\-enrich\-net\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-\-ndjson\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-raw\-cmdline\fR]
[\fB\-\-debug\-log\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B weird
\fBinspect\fR \fIPID\fR
.br
.B weird
\fBtrust\fR \fINAME_OR_PATH\fR
.br
.B weird
\fBrules\fR [\fBshow\fR | \fBinit\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]]
.br
.B weird
\fBcache\fR [\fB\-\-rebuild\fR]
.br
.B weird
\fBbaseline\fR \fBinit\fR | \fBshow\fR | \fBdiff\fR
.br
.B weird
\fBwatch\fR
.br
.B weird
\fBfalco\-rules\fR
.br
.B weird
\fBman\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B weird
scans running processes and highlights suspicious behavior and unclear provenance.
It combines process inventory with package manager ownership, signature evidence,
and behavioral signals to produce a weirdness score and trust label.
The output is deterministic where possible (normalized cmdline, stable sorting)
and can be emitted as JSON/NDJSON for automation.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
Data flow (best effort, never hard-fails):
.TP
.B Collectors
Process inventory: osquery (preferred) or /proc fallback. Network inventory: ss (preferred) or /proc/net fallback.
.TP
.B Signals
Deleted executable, memfd/anonymous exec, suspicious path, random name, bare interpreter, ELF sanity.
.TP
.B Provenance
Package manager ownership + signature evidence (two-axis model).
.TP
.B Policy
Scoring weights and thresholds from config.toml; toggles for higher-noise checks.
.TP
.B Rules
rules.toml allows suppress/downgrade/upgrade/tag with profiles.
.TP
.B Output
Table or JSON/NDJSON; optional baseline/diff.
.TP
.B Partial errors
Collector failures are reported and return exit code 3 when applicable.
.SH DETERMINISM
Process keys are derived from realpath + uid + ppid + normalized cmdline.
Cmdline whitespace is normalized across collectors, and output is sorted to reduce noise.
Network connection lists are sorted in detail output for stability.
.SH PROVENANCE MODEL
Two-axis provenance:
.TP
.B Ownership
Owned / unowned / unknown (package manager ownership only).
.TP
.B Signature
official / third_party / unverified / unknown.
.PP
Trust levels are mapped conservatively. APT with official keyring match -> OFFICIAL.
APT signed-by non-distro -> THIRD_PARTY. Other managers only elevate if verification is explicit.
.SH SIGNATURE VERIFICATION
.TP
.B APT
Uses InRelease + keyring fingerprints (strongest support).
.TP
.B RPM (DNF/YUM/Zypper)
Uses rpmkeys/rpm --checksig when cached RPM is available; otherwise reports unavailable.
.TP
.B Pacman
Reads SigLevel policy; per-package verification requires pkg+sig and is not performed.
.TP
.B APK
Uses apk verify for integrity (does not claim signature verification).
.TP
.B XBPS / Portage / swupd
Reports signed-repo/manifest evidence only (no per-package verification).
.TP
.B Nix
Tries nix store verify --no-contents when available; otherwise integrity-only.
.SH RULES
rules.toml supports profiles and actions: suppress, downgrade, upgrade, tag.
Run \fBweird rules init\fR to create a template.
.SH BASELINE / DIFF
Baseline stores process_key, exe realpath, sha256 (if readable), uid, cmdline (sanitized),
package, trust level, provenance, and evidence snippet. Diff reports new, changed_hash,
moved, and trust_changed.
.SH NETWORK ENRICHMENT
Network view uses outbound connections by default. Private/loopback/link-local addresses
can be filtered. \fB--include-listen\fR adds listening sockets. \fB--enrich-net\fR enables
best-effort reverse DNS and simple context hints.
.SH SECURITY & PRIVACY
Cmdline is sanitized by default in JSON/NDJSON and baseline outputs; use \fB--raw-cmdline\fR
to include raw cmdline (may contain secrets). Subprocesses are time-bounded to avoid hangs.
.SH HARDENING
.TP
.B Falco execution
Uses absolute paths (/usr/bin/sudo, /usr/bin/falco) to prevent PATH manipulation.
.TP
.B Cache integrity
Cache is written with 0600 permissions, ownership checks, and a checksum; atomic writes.
.TP
.B Symlink validation
Symlink resolution is validated against allowed base directories to prevent escapes.
.TP
.B Debug logging
Enable with \fB--debug-log FILE\fR or WEIRD_DEBUG_LOG to troubleshoot failures.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (default)
Scan for suspicious processes. Shows a table and (by default) detail for
high\-score and unknown trust levels.
.TP
.B inspect \fIPID\fR
Show a detailed inspection of a specific process, including open files and a
process tree when available.
.TP
.B trust \fINAME_OR_PATH\fR
Add a process name or path to the user trusted list.
.TP
.B rules show
Print a rules template.
.TP
.B rules init
Write the rules template to disk. Use \fB\-\-force\fR to overwrite.
.TP
.B cache
Show cache status. Use \fB\-\-rebuild\fR to rebuild file\-\>package mapping.
.TP
.B baseline
Baseline/diff mode. \fBinit\fR writes a baseline, \fBshow\fR prints it, \fBdiff\fR compares.
.TP
.B watch
Run Falco in the foreground with weird rules (requires /usr/bin/falco and sudo).
.TP
.B falco\-rules
Print Falco rules for integration.
.TP
.B man
Show the manual page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Show all processes, not just suspicious ones.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Include containers, Flatpak, and Snap processes.
.TP
.B \-\-net
Focus on processes with network activity.
.TP
.B \-\-net\-outbound\-only
Only include outbound connections (default).
.TP
.B \-\-include\-listen
Include listening sockets in network view.
.TP
.B \-\-enrich\-net
Enable network enrichment (best effort).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-ndjson
Output newline\-delimited JSON (one process per line).
.TP
.B \-\-log\-file \fIFILE\fR
Append logs to a file (no secrets).
.TP
.B \-\-raw\-cmdline
Include raw cmdline in JSON/NDJSON output (may contain secrets).
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-log \fIFILE\fR
Enable debug logging to FILE for troubleshooting.
.SH OUTPUT
The table shows PID, weirdness score, trust level, process name, and a short
description. If no description is found for an UNKNOWN process, the table
shows "Likely Malware".
.SH TRUST LEVELS
Trust levels indicate provenance, not security. Examples:
.TP
.B OFFICIAL
Signed distro repository.
.TP
.B THIRD_PARTY
From APT but signed by a non\-distro key.
.TP
.B UNTRACKED_KEY
Key is trusted but origin is unclear.
.TP
.B USER_LOCAL
User\-installed binary (e.g., /usr/local or ~/.local).
.TP
.B APPIMAGE
Portable AppImage (no sandbox).
.TP
.B FLATPAK, SNAP, CONTAINER, KERNEL, USER_TRUSTED, UNKNOWN
Other provenance classifications.
.SH WEIRDNESS LABELS
For UNKNOWN, UNTRACKED_KEY, USER_LOCAL, and APPIMAGE, weirdness labels are
graduated by score:
.TP
.B 0
UNKNOWN (green)
.TP
.B 1\-19
UNKNOWN (A little weird) (yellow)
.TP
.B 20\-39
UNKNOWN (Weird) (magenta)
.TP
.B 40\-59
UNKNOWN (Very weird) (red)
.TP
.B 60+
UNKNOWN (CRITICAL WEIRDNESS) (bold red)
.PP
Trusted processes with nonzero weirdness display "(Maybe Weird)".
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/config.toml
Policy configuration.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/rules.toml
User rules.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/user_trusted.txt
User trusted list.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/cache/file_package_map.json
File\-\>package cache.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/desc_cache.json
Description cache for man/registry lookups.
.TP
.B ~/.config/weird/baseline.json
Baseline data.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No alerts above threshold.
.TP
.B 1
Warning.
.TP
.B 2
Critical.
.TP
.B 3
Internal error / partial failure.
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Trust indicates provenance only. Even OFFICIAL packages can be compromised.
Use weird as part of defense\-in\-depth, not as your only security control.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
weird
Scan for suspicious processes.
.TP
weird \-v
Show all processes.
.TP
weird \-\-net
Focus on networked processes.
.TP
weird inspect 1234
Inspect a specific PID.
.TP
weird rules init
Create a rules file template.
.TP
weird man
Show the manual page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR ss (8),
.BR lsof (8)
